class Solution:
    def setZeroes(self, matrix: List[List[int]]) -> None:
        #row
        m=len(matrix)
        #column
        n=len(matrix[0])
        
        m_row=[0]*m
        n_row=[0]*n
        for i in range(m):
            for j in range(n):
                if matrix[i][j]==0:
                    m_row[i]=1
                    n_row[j]=1
        for i in range(m):
            for j in range(n):
                if m_row[i]==1 or n_row[j]==1:
                    matrix[i][j]=0
        return matrix


  Idea
Use two arrays:
row[] → marks rows containing 0
col[] → marks columns containing 0
Algorithm
Traverse the matrix.
If matrix[i][j] == 0:
row[i] = 1
col[j] = 1
Traverse again.

If row[i] == 1 or col[j] == 1, set:

matrix[i][j] = 0
Why 2 Passes?
1st Pass: Record which rows/columns need to be zero.
2nd Pass: Update the matrix.
Avoids creating new zeros that would incorrectly affect other cells.
Time Complexity
First traversal: O(n × m)
Second traversal: O(n × m)
Overall: O(n × m)
Space Complexity
row[] → O(n)
col[] → O(m)
Overall: O(n + m)
Interview One-Liner

Mark all rows and columns containing 0 using two arrays, then make those rows and columns zero in a second traversal.
